/* R12 — LN PO_Update response → the closed contract { erpKey, erpStatus, message?, correlationBag? }.
   Shape verified against the live DEM endpoint 2026-07-29 (probe runs 1-3):
     { "PurchaseOrder": [ { "Header": { OrderNo, Status, Remarks, ... }, "Line": [ { LineNo, SeqNo, Status, Remarks } ] } ] }

   erpKey = Header.OrderNo. A PO response creates no new ERP document, so the order number IS the handle;
   the dispatcher writes it to OutboxMessage.ErpCode, which Acks the row synchronously (D11) rather than
   leaving it Dispatched awaiting an inbound erp-ack.

   A2 — per-line Status is REPORTED, never fatal: Header.Status alone decides pass/fail (the code-owned
   predicate in LnDynamicDispatcher reads erpStatus). Failing lines are folded into the message so a header
   "Success" hiding a rejected line is still visible in InforSyncLog.

   !! Do not read Status:"Success" as proof the update applied. Probe run 3 sent POStatus:"Zzz" and got
   Status:"Success" back — LN does not validate the field. This mapping extracts what the envelope offers;
   it cannot certify more than that.

   PurchaseOrder[0] is safe on a singleton: JSONata index-selects the first item whether or not the path
   result collapsed to a bare object. */
(
    $po := PurchaseOrder[0];
    $failed := [$po.Line[Status != "Success"]];
    $lineText := $count($failed) = 0 ? "" : " [line failures: " & $join($failed.(LineNo & "/" & SeqNo), ", ") & "]";
    $msg := ($po.Header.Remarks ? $po.Header.Remarks : "") & $lineText;
    {
        "erpKey": $string($po.Header.OrderNo),
        "erpStatus": $string($po.Header.Status),
        "message": $msg != "" ? $msg
    }
)
